        {
            "block_label": "paragraph_title",
            "block_content": "### 1. Market Microstructure Structure",
            "block_bbox": [
                163,
                963,
                533,
                987
            ],
            "block_id": 4,
            "block_order": 1,
            "group_id": 4,
            "block_polygon_points": [
                [
                    163.0,
                    963.0
                ],
                [
                    533.0,
                    963.0
                ],
                [
                    533.0,
                    987.0
                ],
                [
                    163.0,
                    987.0
                ]
            ]
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "● We follow the standard Kyle (1985) approach, assuming a single risky asset with liquidation value v, traded by one informed trader, a continuum (or aggregate) of noise traders, and a competitive market maker who sets price as a function of observed order flow.",
            "block_bbox": [
                212,
                1003,
                1082,
                1077
            ],
            "block_id": 5,
            "block_order": 2,
            "group_id": 5,
            "block_polygon_points": [
                [
                    212.0,
                    1003.0
                ],
                [
                    1082.0,
                    1003.0
                ],
                [
                    1082.0,
                    1077.0
                ],
                [
                    212.0,
                    1077.0
                ]
            ]
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "- In contrast to classical structural models that decompose firm value into an \"asset component\" and a \"debt component,\" our framework decomposes observed trading activity into an informed component x and a noise component u, with only their sum  $ y = x + u $ observable to the market maker.",
            "block_bbox": [
                211,
                1083,
                1083,
                1180
            ],
            "block_id": 6,
            "block_order": 3,
            "group_id": 6,
            "block_polygon_points": [
                [
                    211.0,
                    1083.0
                ],
                [
                    1083.0,
                    1083.0
                ],
                [
                    1083.0,
                    1180.0
                ],
                [
                    211.0,
                    1180.0
                ]
            ]
        },
        {
            "block_label": "paragraph_title",
            "block_content": "### 2. Risk-Neutral Market Maker and Linear Pricing Rule",
            "block_bbox": [
                162,
                1195,
                730,
                1220
            ],
            "block_id": 7,
            "block_order": 4,
            "group_id": 7,
            "block_polygon_points": [
                [
                    162.0,
                    1195.0
                ],
                [
                    730.0,
                    1195.0
                ],
                [
                    730.0,
                    1220.0
                ],
                [
                    162.0,
                    1220.0
                ]
            ]
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "- Consistent with Kyle (1985), we assume a risk-neutral, competitive market maker who sets the price equal to the conditional expectation of liquidation value given observed order flow,  $ p = \\mathbb{E}[v \\mid y] $, earning zero expected profit in equilibrium.",
            "block_bbox": [
                212,
                1235,
                1082,
                1309
            ],
            "block_id": 8,
            "block_order": 5,
            "group_id": 8,
            "block_polygon_points": [
                [
                    212.0,
                    1235.0
                ],
                [
                    1082.0,
                    1235.0
                ],
                [
                    1082.0,
                    1309.0
                ],
                [
                    212.0,
                    1309.0
                ]
            ]
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "- We restrict attention to linear equilibria, in which the informed trader's order is linear in her signal  $ (x = \\beta(v - p_0)) $ and the market maker's pricing rule is linear in order flow  $ (p = p_0 + \\lambda y) $. This is the standard equilibrium refinement in the microstructure literature, applied across single- and multi-period settings with one or many informed traders (Kyle, 1985; Admati and Pfleiderer, 1988; Holden and Subrahmanyam, 1992; Foster and Viswanathan, 1996; Back, 1992), and whose",
            "block_bbox": [
